  "id": "polish_american_polka",
  "name": "Polish-American polka (Chicago honky and Eastern styles)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Dance music of Polish-American communities in Chicago, Buffalo, Detroit, Connecticut and the Northeast. The Chicago 'honky' or 'push' style, launched in the postwar years by Li'l Wally Jagiello's records on his own Jay Jay label, slowed the tempo and centred a Chemnitzer concertina, one or two trumpets, bass and drums behind singing in Polish and English, and was later shaped by Eddie Blazonczyk's Versatones; the brassier, faster Eastern style favoured clarinets and trumpet sections in tight harmony. The concertina card stands in for the Chemnitzer.",
  "recipe": "classic North American mid-1950s polish-american polka, drinking Song, americas branch. concertina, schilke chicago lead bright trumpet, clarinet, upright bass, brushes-and-sticks drum kit. dry but alive 1948-1965 indie-commercial early tape us small. passive ribbon, tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips.",
